Don't wait half a second for a window style change that changes nothing on Android.

SDL_CreateWindow() sets the window out of fullscreen mode (ApplyWindowFlags), and on Android
Android_SetWindowFullscreen() calls setWindowStyle(false) with the activity lock held. Leaving
fullscreen, SDLActivity waited up to 500 ms for surfaceChanged() whenever the surface was as big
as the display, taking that for fullscreen mode. A window laid out edge to edge (under the
status and navigation bars, as Android 15 lays out apps) is that big without fullscreen mode;
it keeps its size, so no surfaceChanged() came. And the window gaining focus meanwhile called
nativeFocusChanged() on the UI thread, which waits for the activity lock: so every start of an
app spent the half second with both threads waiting (on a Redmi Note 9 Pro, from the window
shown to its first frame).

It waits now only when fullscreen mode is on, the one case leaving it changes the layout.

Kompoz applies this to SDL 3.4.18 (scripts/fetch-deps.sh).

--- a/android-project/app/src/main/java/org/libsdl/app/SDLActivity.java
+++ b/android-project/app/src/main/java/org/libsdl/app/SDLActivity.java
@@ -1025,7 +1025,10 @@
                     // If we're laid out fullscreen (even if the status bar and nav bar are present),
                     // or are actively in fullscreen, we're going to change size and should wait for
                     // surfaceChanged before we return, so the size is right back in native code.
-                    bShouldWait = bFullscreenLayout;
+                    // Not when fullscreen mode isn't on: a window laid out edge to edge, under the
+                    // bars, keeps its size then, and the wait (the activity lock held, which
+                    // nativeFocusChanged() on this thread waits for) ran out its half second.
+                    bShouldWait = bFullscreenLayout && mFullscreenModeActive;
                 }
             }
 
